// REJECTED — G-MODEL-PLACE: an action that routes the `confidential` origin to
// a role declared `off_device` does not compile, and the diagnostic names the
// action, the origin and the role. This is roadmap item 512's exit test
// verbatim (`docs/design/531-model-placement.md` section 3.1).
//
// A `model role` is a DECLARED PLACEMENT: a name plus where a call to it runs.
// `route model on <action>` places one action's model calls by the origin class
// of what the action is given, reusing item 249's lattice rather than inventing
// a second vocabulary. The arm below is the one sentence the item exists to
// refuse — it says, in the program, that a confidential input leaves the
// device.
//
// The fix the hint offers is not "drop the arm and write `*`". Dropping it
// works, but for the reason section 2.1 of the design note gives rather than
// the one an author expects: `*` is DEFINED not to cover a confidentiality
// origin, so an unnamed `confidential` is not placed at all. `* -> cloud` can
// therefore never be the sentence that sends a confidential input off the
// device.
//
// This file is also the self-host's reproducer. Before slice 3 the gate built
// from `selfhost/lower.rvl` answered every `route model` program
// `BAD|unexpected token at top level`, a generic parse refusal, which is why
// item 512 shipped with an `ACKNOWLEDGED` entry in `tools/tier_guarantees.py`
// instead of a fixture here. The gate now spells the refusal below byte for
// byte and tags it `MODEL`, so the fixture belongs in the corpus and the
// acknowledgement is gone.
//
// expected error:
//   routes the `confidential` origin to model role `cloud`

model role local on_device
model role cloud off_device

service Answer { fn classify(text: Str) -> Str }

component Classifier provides out: Answer {
  route model on classify {
    confidential -> cloud
  }
  provide out { fn classify(text) = text }
}
